WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.780
We've got you down for 10 minutes. If you can just leave some time at the end for

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:04.300
questions.

3
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:05.900
No problem at all.

4
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:06.880
Over to you.

5
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.460
Kia ora koutou. Ko Lisa Pizzoni toku ingoa.

6
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.040
I'm the founder and lead architect of the Indigenous Data Authority,

7
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.460
and I'm here in support of my written submission.

8
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.280
I won't repeat the submission today, but I do want to focus on three things:

9
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.600
the evidence for this bill, the distinction between association and

10
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.340
wrongdoing, and the impact this legislation may have on Māori

11
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.740
communities and providers.

12
00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.420
I want to start by being very clear.

13
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:36.400
I do not support public money being used to fund criminal activity or

14
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.260
deliberately benefiting gangs. But before Parliament creates

15
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.200
new legal obligations and criminal liability, I think it

16
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.380
needs to clearly establish the problem it is trying to solve.

17
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.820
The explanatory material tells us there is concern that

18
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.300
public money could end up in the hands of gangs.

19
00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.500
My question is, where is the evidence showing the scale of that

20
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.220
problem? How often has it actually happened, and how much public money

21
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.121
has been actually diverted?

22
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:08.300
What existing safeguards have failed, and what evidence

23
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.140
shows that this legislative response is necessary and

24
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.080
proportionate? These are important questions when consequence of getting

25
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.200
it wrong may include criminal liability.

26
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:24.400
If Kahukura is part of the justification for this concern, then

27
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.340
I think we also need to look at the whole story.

28
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:31.800
The Auditor-General examined that funding decision and found

29
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.380
that the funding process had been followed.

30
00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.660
The application went through the required assessment process, and the contract

31
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:42.500
contained controls for governance, monitoring, reporting, and audit.

32
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.300
The Auditor-General also found no evidence that the political views of

33
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.980
H2R's co-director influenced the funding decision.

34
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:55.720
So simply pointing to a gang connection does not establish that

35
00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.700
public money was misused. And that brings me to what I think is one of the

36
00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.140
most important principles here. Association may

37
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.620
justify scrutiny. It does not establish wrongdoing.

38
00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.600
We understand that principle elsewhere.

39
00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:13.640
We do not automatically conclude that a political donation

40
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:17.220
proves improper influence. We do not automatically

41
00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.940
conclude that a relationship with a decision-maker proves

42
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.980
corruption. And when we look at who may benefit from legislation

43
00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.880
progressed through processes such as fast track,

44
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.740
association can raise legitimate questions, but

45
00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.060
questions still require evidence.

46
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.580
The same standard should apply here.

47
00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.920
Association is not evidence of misuse, connection is not

48
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.980
evidence of diversion, and concern is not, by itself, an

49
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.520
evidential basis for criminal liability.

50
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.840
If association is enough to trigger scrutiny in one context, it

51
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.140
cannot suddenly become proof in another.

52
00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.320
And that is also why I referred to churches and

53
00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:01.760
faith-based institutes in my written

54
00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:02.420
submission.

55
00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:07.020
I want to be very clear. I'm not saying churches are gangs.

56
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.180
I'm talking about consistency.

57
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.520
The Royal Commission into Abuse and Care documented serious and

58
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.640
widespread abuse within state and faith-based

59
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.440
institutions, including institutional failures and

60
00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.200
failures of accountability. Some of that conduct was

61
00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:28.160
criminal, yet we do not say that every organization connected to

62
00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:31.860
a church should therefore be treated as suspect or excluded from

63
00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.620
public funding. Nor should we. We look at who was

64
00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:39.480
responsible, we look at conduct, we look at control, and we look at the

65
00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:43.380
evidence. I'm simply asking Parliament to apply the same

66
00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:47.340
evidential principle here, because this bill will not operate

67
00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:51.320
in a vacuum. Māori are disproportionately represented in the

68
00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:55.040
criminal justice system and in gang-related settings,

69
00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:59.480
and that means the consequences of this legislation are likely to fall

70
00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.240
disproportionately within Māori communities and on

71
00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.560
organizations working directly with those whānau.

72
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.500
There is a fundamental difference between an organization being controlled by

73
00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.300
a gang and an organization working with people who have gang

74
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.380
affiliations. And they're not the same thing.

75
00:04:18.899 --> 00:04:22.220
If the law does not make that distinction clearly enough, government

76
00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:26.140
agencies will need to manage the risk, and sometimes the easiest

77
00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.980
way to manage that risk is simply don't fund it.

78
00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:35.660
That is the chilling effect I'm concerned about because the organizations

79
00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:39.140
affected may not be supporting criminal activity at all.

80
00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:43.120
They may be trying to reduce it. The New Zealand Law Society

81
00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.480
has also raised concerns about the workability of this bill,

82
00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.800
including uncertainty around who could potentially be criminally

83
00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:54.480
liable and how far the due diligence obligations

84
00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:55.240
extend.

85
00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.120
If Parliament is going to create criminal liability, people should not have to

86
00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.000
guess where that liability begins and ends.

87
00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:05.380
So my position is quite straightforward.

88
00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:09.440
If public money is deliberately being channeled to gangs, then stop it.

89
00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:14.120
If an organization is being controlled by a gang and public money is

90
00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.780
being diverted for criminal benefit, then it needs to be dealt with.

91
00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.560
But establish that through evidence.

92
00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:25.340
Target conduct, target control, target actual

93
00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:29.280
financial benefit. Do not allow association to become a

94
00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:33.120
substitute for evidence, and do not create a law so

95
00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:36.920
uncertain that legitimate Māori and community organizations

96
00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:40.420
working to reduce harm become collateral damage.

97
00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:44.880
I'm asking the committee to either decline this bill in its current form

98
00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:48.720
or substantially amend it. Make the evidential

99
00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:52.580
threshold clear, make the meaning of indirectly

100
00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:56.300
clear, make the due diligence obligation clear, make it

101
00:05:56.380 --> 00:06:00.240
clear who can actually be criminally liable, and ensure

102
00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:03.880
legitimate Māori and community providers are not caught simply

103
00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:07.720
because of the people and communities they're working with.

104
00:06:08.556 --> 00:06:12.036
Public money should not fund criminal organisations.

105
00:06:12.216 --> 00:06:15.996
I agree with that principle. But good legislation must do

106
00:06:16.076 --> 00:06:19.456
more than state a principle. It must establish the problem.

107
00:06:19.676 --> 00:06:22.656
It must respond proportionately to the evidence.

108
00:06:23.076 --> 00:06:27.036
It must be clear about who will carry the consequences when the law gets

109
00:06:27.116 --> 00:06:30.656
that distinction wrong. And for those reasons, I ask the

110
00:06:30.696 --> 00:06:34.116
committee not to recommend this bill in its current form.

111
00:06:34.376 --> 00:06:34.996
Nga mihi.

112
